# Performance

Speed is one of Redlamp's promises, and redlamp.app/performance shows it over time from `docs/performance/history.jsonl`: one record per run, with its commit, machine and load. The metrics, their units and groups are in `docs/performance/metrics.json`. The card at the top of the README is drawn from the same history.

## After work that can change speed or memory

Rendering, decoding, the detail stage, masks, the filmstrip and folders, the Develop panels:

1. On a quiet Mac (nothing else building), run `scripts/perf-record.sh`. It builds Release, runs `redlamp bench` on the CC0 fixtures, the slider sweep (then 30 seconds with the app left alone, for its CPU, wakeups and memory) and, if `/tmp/rl-fixture-50k.noindex` exists, the folders run, appends one record and prints what moved beyond its noise (10%, or the runs' own spread).
2. A metric that got slower: find out why before moving on. Fix it, or, when the cost buys something (quality, correctness), say so in the commit message; the page shows the commit's subject beside the change.
3. Commit the new line of `history.jsonl`, and the README's performance card it redraws, with the change it measures.
4. If the README's "Measured performance" tables quote a figure that moved, update it from the record.

A run under load (a load average above 8) is recorded as noisy: drawn, never compared. Never edit or delete past records; a mistaken run is followed by a correct one.

During an agent wave, parallel builds make every number noisy: wave agents don't record, and name performance-sensitive changes in their final report; the orchestrator records after merging.

## Adding a metric

Add it to `metrics.json` (ID, label, detail, unit, group, which way is better, `measuredBy`), measure it in `redlamp bench` or a harness's `writeMetrics`, and run `scripts/perf-record.sh`. A metric the README recorded before can be backfilled: give it a `readme` entry and run `scripts/perf-backfill.py --apply`, which rewrites only the README records.

## Releases

After each release, `scripts/perf-history.py release <tag> --apply` measures its zip on GitHub and records its download and installed size, as a record from the release (the release room's Records step). Sizes don't depend on a machine or its load, so these records are never noisy.

## The README's card

`scripts/perf-card.py` draws the card at the top of the README (`docs/images/performance-card.svg` and `-light.svg`, and the README's `<picture>` with its alt text) from the history. Its four columns (responsive, memory, CPU, size) each show the first two of their metrics that have been measured, from each metric's latest quiet record on the M1 Ultra (from a release, for sizes); a metric only ever measured under load shows its latest record, marked. `perf-history.py` redraws it whenever it adds a record, and CI's quick checks and the push gate fail when it's stale (`--check`). What the card shows is `COLUMNS` and `CAPTIONS` in the script, which refuses text that would overflow its column.

## On the page

No Lightroom figures: Redlamp hasn't measured Lightroom on the same photos, and publishing its benchmarks waits on DEC-13. Words are plain: "faster", "slower", "less memory", with the figures and dates.

## Check

```bash
scripts/perf-history.py report
scripts/perf-card.py --check
cd web && npm test && npm run build
```
